 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_kQPdXBwRV3szcFmZ",
   "name": "Transforming Relationships in the Form y=ax^n",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "814194038"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Transforming relationships in the form y=ax^n"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I use logarithms to linearise a graph in the form y = ax<sup>n</sup>?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Logarithms can be used to linearise graphs of **power functions**\n- Suppose $y=ax^{n}$\n\n  - You can take logarithms of both sides\n  \n    - $\\mathrm{ln}y=\\mathrm{ln}(ax^{n})$\n  - You can split the right hand side into the sum of two logarithms\n  \n    - $\\mathrm{ln}y=\\mathrm{ln}a+\\mathrm{ln}(x^{n})$\n  - You can bring down the power in the final term\n  \n    - $\\mathrm{ln}y=\\mathrm{ln}a+n\\mathrm{ln}x$\n- $\\mathrm{ln}y=\\mathrm{ln}a+n\\mathrm{ln}x$ is in linear form $Y=mX+c$\n\n  - $Y=\\mathrm{ln}y$\n  - $X=\\mathrm{ln}x$\n  - $m=n$\n  - $c=\\mathrm{ln}a$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How can I use linearised form to find the unknown constants?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- After linearising the function it will be in the form $\\mathrm{ln}y=\\mathrm{ln}a+n\\mathrm{ln}x$\n\n  - $n$ is the gradient of the straight line graph\n  - $\\mathrm{ln}a$ is the $y$-intercept of the straight line graph\n- Once you know the value of the gradient of the straight line graph this is the value of $n$\n- You will need to find the value of $a$ by solving the equation $\\mathrm{ln}a=c$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
